[Event "Knight School"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defence"]
[Variation "Advance Variation, Short Variation"]
[ECO "B12"]
[Character "solid,positional"]
[Risk "low"]
[EarlyMate "none"]
[Tricks "none"]
[Themes "c5-break,good-bishop,attack-the-base"]
[Kind "main"]

1. e4 c6 { Prepares ...d5 with a pawn behind it, so the centre pawn is always defended. Black gets a solid centre without shutting in the c8 bishop. }
2. d4 d5 { Challenges the pawn on e4. The c6 pawn guards d5, so Black can recapture there with a pawn. }
3. e5 Bf5 { Brings the bishop out before ...e6 closes its diagonal. This is the whole point of the Caro-Kann: Black never ends up with a bad bishop. }
4. Nf3 e6 { With the bishop safely outside the pawn chain, ...e6 can support d5. }
5. Be2 ( 5. c3 c5 6. Be2 Nc6 7. O-O ) ( 5. Nc3 c5 6. Be2 Nc6 7. O-O ) ( 5. Bd3 Bxd3 6. Qxd3 c5 7. dxc5 Bxc5 )
5... c5 { Attacks the base of White's pawn chain at d4. If that pawn goes, the pawn on e5 loses its support. }
6. Be3 ( 6. dxc5 Bxc5 7. O-O Ne7 )
6... cxd4 { Opens the c-file and gains time: White must recapture, and Black can then attack the piece that took. }
7. Nxd4 ( 7. Bxd4 Nc6 8. Bb5 )
7... Ne7 { If White takes on f5, the knight recaptures. That keeps Black's pawns healthy, where ...exf5 would double them. }
8. Nd2 Nbc6 { Develops and attacks d4 again, so White must keep guarding it. }
9. N2f3 Bg4 { Attacks the knight on f3, which guards both d4 and e5. Trading it loosens White's grip on the centre. }
10. O-O Bxf3 { Takes the knight that guards e5 and d4. The bishop has done its job, and the e5 pawn loses a protector. }
11. Nxf3 Nf5 { Heads for d4 and hits the bishop on e3. If White leaves the book, trade pieces, castle after ...Be7, and keep aiming at d4 and e5. }
*
